Only the template version. So `Exiv2::toString(buf.data())` with `buf.data()` being `uint8_t*` would print the pointer address. No READ overflow there.

Let me reconsider. Maybe the vulnerability is in a different handler. Let me look at the `smhd` handler in the vulnerable version again:

```cpp
else if (equalsQTimeTag(buf, "smhd")) {
    io_->read(buf.data(), 4);
    io_->read(buf.data(), 4);
    xmpData_["Xmp.audio.Balance"] = returnBufValue(buf, 2);
}
```

`buf` is 5 bytes. `io_->read(buf.data(), 4)` reads 4 bytes into buf[0..3]. Then `io_->read(buf.data(), 4)` reads 4 more bytes into buf[0..3]. Then `returnBufValue(buf, 2)` reads buf[0] and buf[1]. That's fine.

